and known as the provincial relations secretariat is being formed in the privy council office. It reports to the Prime Minister himself. This new secretariat, in 20220-203i
12, 1964	3205
Proceedings on Adjournment Motion addition to advising the Prime Minister, will perform a co-ordinating and servicing role, not an executive one or an operational one. It will not displace such units as are responsible for those aspects of federal-provincial relations which fall clearly within the jurisdiction of the different departments.
I have here, Mr. Speaker, the terms of reference of this unit and I will simply skim through them. They are as follows:
1.	To keep in touch with, review, and bring to the attention of the Prime Minister, ministers or departments any aspects or implications of present or proposed government policies which would be of interest or concern to the governments of the provinces:
2.	To participate in departmental or interdepartmental consultations and studies;
3.	To provide a point of co-ordination and information on activities of the various federal government departments responsible for particular aspects of federal-provincial relations;
4.	To provide a central point of contact to the provinces;
5.	To assist in federal-provincial consultation and negotiations, as directed by the Prime Minister from time to time;
6.	To provide a secretariat for cabinet and interdepartmental committees on federal-provincial relations; and
7.	In relation to federal-provincial conferences:
(a)	to co-ordinate preparations and to provide the secretariat for federal-provincial conferences;
(b)	to develop procedures, in consultation with other departments concerned;
(c)	to attend ministerial or other federal-provincial conferences;
(d)	to assist other departments in respect of other arrangements and preparations for federal-provincial conferences and meetings.
It is hoped, Mr. Speaker, that the provinces individually will make similar arrangements. The province of Quebec established a department of federal-provincial affairs in 1960.
